<article class="env-lemma" id="08YK">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08YK">Lemma <span data-tag="08YK">5.26.3</span>.</a> Let $X$ be an extremally disconnected space. If $U, V \subset X$ are disjoint open subsets, then $\overline{U}$ and $\overline{V}$ are disjoint too. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By assumption $\overline{U}$ is open, hence $V \cap \overline{U}$ is open and disjoint from $U$, hence empty because $\overline{U}$ is the intersection of all the closed subsets of $X$ containing $U$. This means the open $\overline{V} \cap \overline{U}$ avoids $V$ hence is empty by the same argument. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>